etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4189195\nCREATED: 2026-06-13\nDOMAIN: emirates247.com\nSLUG: 2564\nTEXT: The Tata iPhone parts factory in India has been accused of polluting farmland and groundwater by the Tamil Nadu Pollution Control Board. The factory, which is a supplier to Apple, was found to be discharging wastewater into a rainwater harvesting pond that overflowed and contaminated groundwater in adjacent agricultural lands. Despite complaints from local farmers for months, Tata had not taken any corrective actions by the time of a regulatory warning notice issued on May 25. The board is now threatening to shut down the factory unless Tata explains why it should not be cut off from power. This is part of a series of issues affecting Apple\'s India supply chain, including a fire at the factory in 2024 and allegations of labor practices at another supplier.\n\nID: 4189837\nCREATED: 2026-06-13\nDOMAIN: gulftoday.ae\nSLUG: tatas-iphone-parts-factory-contaminated-farmland-water-india-pollution-body-alleges\nTEXT: Tata\'s iPhone parts factory in India has been found to have contaminated farmland and water due to wastewater discharge. The pollution board\'s warning notice, dated May 25, states that Tata discharged wastewater into a rainwater harvesting pond inside its facility, causing it to overflow and contaminate groundwater in adjacent agricultural lands. Despite previous instructions from the pollution board, Tata failed to take corrective actions by December 23, 2025. The company claims it is "in full compliance with all regulatory norms" after conducting an independent analysis, but the pollution board has asked Tata to explain why power to the unit should not be cut and the unit closed.\n\n\n',)
